nce between the  objective  and the sample we also have  a family of upright microscopes like the  thunder image or tissue for slide work  and if you desire to take z-stacks  for some ear slides we have the thunder  tissue 3d  or it's the thunder imager 3d tissue  we also have a family of inverted  microscopes  the 3d cell culture which can be  enclosed with an incubation chamber  to perform 3d live cell and we also have  the thunder imager  3d assay so all of these systems are  equipped with the thunder  technology  throughout this presentation we have  shown you a few images  of what thunder can accomplish and so  we'll continue with that theme  and show you a few more here is an image  of a mouse  long organoid imaged with a 20x  air objective through one millimeter  plastic bottom dish  rendered in 3d this is the image  prior to thunder  and  this is the image after thunder  here is a mouse brain tissue stained  with  gfap and six four seven  this is a 120 micron z stack  and this image is also rendered in 3d  this is the thunder data set  and that's the raw  here's an image of a mouse lung stained  for the at1  lineage this was a 70 micron thick  z stack also rendered in 3d  this is on the left-hand side the raw  data set and on the right hand side  is the thunder data set and this was  processed using  large volume computational clearing or  lvcc  and lastly here's an uncleared human  brain organoid  it was a two by one tile scan there was  four  fluorescent channels and a total z stack  thickness was 285 microns  and this image is a result after  using lvcc  so now we've discussed our thunder  technology  let's see how it's actually applied to  our instrumentation  we've given some examples but we want to  go through some specific hardware  examples that you can use with the  thunder so here we have our 3d cell  culture  it's based on our inverted dmi8 platform  we compare cameras motorized stages and  other things with it  to really give you a high end microscope  so one of the biggest choices any  researcher needs to make  when doing microscopy is to choose the  correct objectives  at leica microsystems we have many  different objectives to choose from  we have water immersion with a micro  dispenser for long-term imaging  motorized correction collars for doing  multi-photon  we even have water or glycerol  objectives to match refractive indices  of your samples we also have ones that  go up to  bab and other types of clearing methods  we also have many with correction  collars for allowing different thickness  of  sample holder to image through so while  we have all of these different options  it may be a little hard to find the one  you need so we've actually come out with  what we call objective finder  and this allows you to search through  our extensive catalog  of objectives and really choose the  objective that's correct for you  and your application so beyond  objective selection the other benefit  you have from going with a  compound microscope like the dmi8  is the ability to use real-time control  of the system  so in many systems when you have a usb  controlled system your illumination will  turn on  it'll tell it to take the exposure and  then it will tell it to turn the  illumination off this time here is  what's needed to communicate that back  and forth in the system  when using a truly synced or  synchronized system like the dmi in our  synapse controller  when we tell the illumination to turn on  and when to expose everything is ttl  triggered  so that our illumination is in line with  our exposure and we are not over  exposing our samples so this is called  synapse  it allows for ttl triggering we can do  this with our  stages our led light sources and even  third-party components so we can control  things not sold by leica as well  this allows for high-speed acquisition  by synchronizing the camera with the  illumination  the synchronization also allows for  minimized light exposure  which minimizes or reduces photo damage  and bleaching of the sample  also on our dmi8 is something that  allows us to create  and keep focus we call this  adaptive focus control it is  a way of locking the objective to the  sample to minimize z  drift due to temperature fluctuations or  mechanical drift  the way this works is that we have a far  red  led light that comes in through the  objective  so being far red it does not expose your  sample to unneeded light  it looks for the back reflection off of  your sample  it works with air and multi-immersion  objectives  so you are not restricted to air or oil  you can bridge all of those  it will lock your focus to the cell  or your sample and then as time  progresses and you have fluctuations in  the system let's say your  sample heats up a bit and it shifts the  focus  up higher without afc your focus would  be now out of focus  but with afc the objective will follow  that thermal fluctuation  and keep the cell in your sample in  focus  so an example of how this works over  time  we have live cell imaging being shown  here  so we're able to keep the cells in focus  by having a rigid stand  but also that hardware called the afc  we can also implement software autofocus  as well  what that really allows you to do is do  long term time lapsing so you can see  down here we have c elegant embryos  and we're imaging those over the course  of multiple hours  we also have cell viability essays  where we're imaging over multiple hours  as well and tracking cell development  and motion  so along with this high-speed imaging in  this afc  we also have stages that allow for  multi-well play imaging  and give you high speed more data points  and overall better statistics because  now you have many more data points  so we can capture capture samples that  go from either slides to well plates so  chamber slides  multi-well plates things like that  through  our custom carriers in our software and  this allows for  automated imaging of those carriers  where you're not setting up by hand  a 96 well play acquisition  with thunder we then have that available  in real time through our computational  clearing and adaptive deconvolution  where as we collect our data at high  speed  we get our end result at high speed as  well  so i've mentioned this custom carrier  this is all  implemented through our software within  las x  it's called navigator this allows you to  identify  areas of interest within las x you can  load  and align and begin imaging with saved  templates  which is being shown here on the right  where they're loading a template  and with that afc and our other focus  control methods we can keep our sample  in focus  over these really large areas so you can  see he's  implemented afc here and now as it goes  along in images in each one of these  chamber slides  the sample is in focus and he's  collecting quality data  so with navigator you can get these very  large mosaic  images of tissue sections you can also  do  multi-weld experiments multi-position  experiments  so here is what we would do with a mouse  slice or a slice of mouth spraying  where we have our thunder image on the  left our raw on the right  so this is a multi-tile acquisition  where we've taken  thunder and then stitched it as a post  process  so to give you a few more examples of  the kind of data you can get off of this  system  we have here a transgenic  prenatal five-day mouse heart that's  expressing  gfp and you can see that we can get a  very large three-dimensional image  with our 10x objective using large  volume computational clearing  and the full thickness of this sample is  740 microns  another example using an organoid this  is actually an organoid cluster stained  with dapi  for the nucleus gfp for the plasma  membrane  and you can see we can get the very  large  overall structure as well as all the  fine structure within that  and this was done with large volume  computational clearing as well  our next example is an adult mouse ovary  that's been fixed and stained with  anti-male box and then cleared with  three  disco you can see that we have  very clear image quality after  deconvolution  this is a very thick sample taken at  almost 600 microns  with our 10x objective  the next example is a whole mouse embryo  stained with beta-3 tubulin and cleared  with bad  this is actually taken with a bab  multi-immersion  refractive index matching objective a  20x multi-immersion objective  so you can see we have a very thick  section  it's 700 microns it consists of 32  stitch tiles  and you can see before thunder we have a  lot of out of focus light  and then after thunder our image quality  is greatly improved with large volume  computational clearing  the next example was mouse brain express  expressing ampk in red and the microglia  with ib one plus in green  it was taken with a 40x 1.3 n a  objective  and both of these images are maximum  projections taken from a 27 micron thick  3d 3d volume you can see with the raw  we have a lot of out of focus light as  well as blur just from  the inherent wide field imaging after  large volume computational clearing  we remove that out of focus light and  enhance our image quality and resolution  this can also be applied to live cell  imaging as well so our next example is  actually a 48 hour  time lapse of live cells being imaged  across  multiple wells this is an explant  tissue of abdominal aorta  it's genetically expressing td tomato  and when a transcription event occurs it  begins expressing  egfp so in our magenta channel is our td  tomato  and you can see the cells that are in  cyan that is now our egfp  and you can see over the course of many  hours going up to  just under 48 hours these cells can be  tracked  and distinguished between td  and egfp  as previously mentioned thunder is  included  in our family of thunder imagers  here's an image of the thunder imager 3d  tissue  it's based on an upright platform  the 3d tissue imager is ideally suited  for imaging  your slide work to see how thunder  can benefit such samples  here are a few examples  here is an image of a neuromuscular  junction  amino stain for neurofilament and beta  bungarotoxin the left hand side of the  image  is the wide field fluorescent image and  the image on the right hand side  is the image after sunder  this is a mouse lung stained for fitzy  psy3 and alexa 633  the image was acquired using a 20x  objective  and the final image was projected into  extended depth of field  or edof the left hand side  is the edof image which is the wide  field image  and the image on the right is the edof  image after thunder  here is an image of a drosophila  rendered in 3d  within our las x software  the stainings were 647 and 555  labeling a subset of motor neurons  here is the image after thunder  and that's the image before thunder  the thunder imager model organism is a  stereo microscope  that is ideally suited for imaging whole  model organisms  such as zebrafish c elegans  drosophila or whole mouse  to see how thunder can be utilized  on such samples here again  are a few examples  this is an image of a cortical spheroid  where red labels neurons and green  labels astrocytes  the image was acquired using a 2x  0.15 na objective  set to a zoom of 3.4  the left hand side is an edof image  taken in wide field fluorescence and the  image on the right hand side  is the edof image after applying thunder  this is an image of a c elegan the  strain was  mah-215  with a dual fluorescent reporter of m  cherry  and gfp the image on the left-hand side  is the wide-field fluorescent image and  the image  on the right hand side is a result  after thunder  here we have an image of a c anemone  stained in rfp and ngfp  notice how you can clearly see gfp  signal around the mouth and in bands  in some of the tentacles this result  after thunder  here is a mouse heart  stained for th positive neurons  it's rendered in 3d within our las x  software  the total z stack thickness was  400 microns here is the result  before thunder and that is the result  after thunder  and lastly this is an image of a mouse  brain  the left hand side is before thunder  and the right-hand side is after  applying thunder  so hopefully with these images we have  shown you you now can appreciate the  power of using thunder  the ability to see through the haze to  increase image contrast and also  image resolution  with that we'll finish on the last and  newest member to the thunder imaging  family  the em cryo clem so the em cryo clem  is an upright based microscope to allow  you to image your samples at cryogenic  temperatures  and then do a coordinate transfer to  your cryoem  or em microscope to then do high  resolution imaging  on those same areas  so the main benefit of the cryo-em  is being able to determine sample  quality and location  before going to the cryo-em to do  high-resolution imaging  so you can image across the grid and  look at your sample quality  identify areas that are not good for  imaging like  crystalline ice contamination and then  identify areas that are good  and would be suitable for em imaging  so you can see here we have intact  meshes with  our cells growing across them  good fluorescent signal we could mark  these and then take them to our cryoen  so as mentioned you can identify and  mark potential targets  so here we have a fluorescence cell of  interest  we could mark it as a coordinate we  could do this across  the entire image or the entire grid  and then once we go to our scanning  electron microscope or our cryoem  we could do a coordinate transfer find  those exact same cells  and image them at high resolution and  even do tomography  another large benefit is being able to  maintain cryo conditions through all of  this process  this maximizes your ability to  successfully find  samples of interest and get good  experimental outcomes  while minimizing the potential of  contamination during loading  and transferring of your sample this is  done by keeping your sample at cryogenic  temperatures  we do this with a cryogenic stage an  objective that works at cryo conditions  and then a shuttle that shuttle your  tran your sample  at cryogenic temperatures between our  system  and your cryo-em with this  and with the fluorescence imaging we've  now added thunder  to the to this as well and on this next  slide you can see as we peel away  we have our raw versus our thundered  image  so with the thunder we get higher  resolution  better image contrast and overall a  better  easier way of marking coordinates for  transferring to the cryoe  these are human cells staying with gfp  and m cherry  and we can target them now with  coordinates that we could transfer  to our cryo-em and then do  high-resolution imaging across them  so that being said we'd like to finish  on two important parts  of our thunder imaging family one being  the publications that have come out so  far for our  instrumentation thunder was launched  in 2019 and since then we've had over 60  publications  using thunder imaging we've taken a  couple selection from that  we have one based on cancer looking at  drug resistance and melanoma  and thunder imaging of cells we have one  in neurobiology  looking at membrane trafficking in brain  slices  using a thunder imager and then last we  have one in  biotechnology looking at conductive  protein dye microfibers  and using a thunder imager to look at  the fluorescence there  so we have this publication list  available on our website  listed here on this link if you'd like  to find it  by searching you can search for leica  microsystems  thunders publication list  and the second piece we'd like to finish  on is quantification  always a major part in any fluorescence  imaging is quantification  this is not an exhaustive look at  quantification but we'd like to give a  few examples of how people can use our  systems  for quantification this first one being  counting so this is a brain slice where  we're looking at neurons  in a mouse brain and we want to count  them but we also want to look at the  co-localization  of our green and red channels so this  image on the bottom left  is our brain slice our our thundered  image  the image on the top right is of  counting those neurons so each one of  these points  is a neuron that has been counted and  then the image below  is looking at the co-localization  between the green red channel where we  can now  count when cells of two different colors  are touching each other  is important for this because when we  apply deconvolution we're getting  better image quality but also better  image resolution which gives you better  localization you'll get better  counting and localization with it  the second one we'd like to show is  ratio metric analysis  this is of an arabidopsis expressing  gfp as we apply  a catalyst peroxide  we can use our sensors to look at the  response of the arabidopsis  and in time we can see how it responds  to that  injection of a of peroxide in the  calcium expression channel  so this is a way of doing ratio metric  analysis with the thunder imagers as  well  again not exhaustive but it is a very  important question that we'd like to  answer  and show so with that we'll  finish with a review of the thunder  imager family  again we have three main instruments  that it's based upon  our male organism which is on our  stereoscope  our 3d tissue on our upright dm6  and our 3d cell culture on our dmi8  the main part or the reason that we  brought thunder  to the customer is to allow for  getting brilliant images fast we also  wanted to allow for effortless work  with new models like organisms  tissue sections and 3d cell cultures  and really allow people to take  advantage of next generation disease  models  not having a restriction to the hardware  they have  so thunder imagers are a new class of  imaging system that rely on  cameras for detection and high quality  hardware  and leica's computational clearing  technology to remove out-of-focus light  and with all of that we hope that you're  able to see through the haze  and see how thunder can benefit you and  your samples  to finish i would like to give you an  idea of who's been talking to you for  the last hour or so  we have vikram coley he's the midwest  advanced workflow specialist  and then you have myself james marr the  mid-atlantic  advanced workflow specialist you can see  that we have our emails listed if you  have any additional questions after this  feel free to reach out we also have a  remote application support line that you  can contact  as well as a youtube channel with  tutorials on how to use our hardware  and our software and with that we'll  take any questions  and thank you so much for joining us for  this webinar have a good day  and thank you so much dr maher and dr  coley for that informative presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar  i would also like to welcome levy velts  who will be joining us  for the q a portion if you have any  questions you'd like to ask  please do so now  ask a question box and we will  directly go ahead and answer as many  questions as we have time for so let's  get started  levy welcome and it looks like we  already have some great questions coming  in our first question  you mentioned the deconvolution  is regulized regulate regularized and  constrained  what is the regularization used  hi uh yeah thank you for these questions  um we've got a few of them here in the  chat and i've tried to respond to some  uh directly uh via via the response so  hopefully you're getting those  um yeah when we think about the  regularization  and constrainment of deconvolution and  what does it mean  either there's there are different  methods that exist  to regularize the deconvolution to to  limit  image noise um to limit artifact  generation  ultimately that the goal here is to  preserve over