ry the analysis to fit your  experiment thank you so much levi  and our next question from an audience  member can thunder be applied  to bf images  no yeah bf so we're what we're talking  about is brightfield  um and in short the answer is no so  thunder is really uh applied to the  fluorescent channels  so within our software system las x  for people using like a micro system  microscopes  um if there is a multi-channel  acquisition set up with a bright field  channel  um the brightfield channel is skipped as  part of the algorithm  so they're all we're only applying  fluorescence i'm sorry we're only  applying  thunder imaging computational clearing  to the fluorescence channels  thank you so much and again i want to  thank our audience for these great  questions  we have time for one more question is  the data  quantifiable so  so james mentioned this um on the last  couple of slides  and and quantifiability again is a  variable  in the community that we we work in  so we can think about is it quantifiable  from a segmentation analysis  analysis perspective is it is it  quantifiable from  a fluorescence intensity measurement  ratio metric analysis all of these  things  but post thunder the data sets can be  quantified for segmentation and cell  identification and cell counting  um fluorescence intensity and and we can  preserve  all of these relative uh fluorescence  intensities so  yes it is quantifiable um and  the the one of the key aspects we have  with our  our thunder imager platform  is you always have the raw  data to to look at and compare to  so when you're measuring peak heights or  what's changed we don't overwrite the  the raw data we don't delete the raw  data the raw data is always there  and then the computational clearing and  the thundering of your samples are all  done  um you know  as a separate module to work with so you  can always  compare and contrast but um but yeah  those are those are all  there's still some other questions in  the cha